\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The pure-gauge selection rule under a central twist}
\date{2026-11-17}

\p{The \vocabk{central cut twist}{fdip-00X4} leaves every
elementary plaquette holonomy unchanged. Its cyclic
transport satisfies
##{h_i(U^{(z)};x)=\rho(z)h_i(U;x),\qquad
 L_i(U^{(z)};x)=\trace_F(\rho(z)h_i(U;x)).}
Suppose explicitly that #{\rho(z)=\chi(z)\bbI_F}
and that the chosen pure-gauge scalar weight satisfies
#{w(U^{(z)})=w(U)}. Then
##{(1-\chi(z))\sum_U w(U)L_i(U;x)=0.}
If multiplication by #{1-\chi(z)} is injective on
#{k}, this numerator vanishes. The normalized
pure-gauge mean vanishes too whenever
#{Z_{\mathrm{pure}}=\sum_Uw(U)} is a unit.}

\proof{
\p{If a plaquette has no edge in direction #{i},
it has no cut factor. If it has an edge in that
direction, its two parallel #{i}-edges start at the
same #{i}-coordinate. They either both miss the cut,
or contribute #{z} and #{z^{-1}} in the two traversal
orientations. Centrality moves these factors next to
one another and cancels them. This also covers a
period of one, with the same labeled-edge convention.}

\p{The positive cyclic path meets the cut exactly
once. Centrality moves its one new factor to the
front of the path product. Applying the representation
and then the trace proves the two transport identities.
A scalar central action factors out of the trace;
centrality alone does not imply scalar action.}

\p{Reindex the finite numerator by the twist
permutation. Weight invariance and the scalar trace
identity give
##{\sum_Uw(U)L_i(U;x)
 =\sum_Uw(U^{(z)})L_i(U^{(z)};x)
 =\chi(z)\sum_Uw(U)L_i(U;x).}
Subtracting proves the selection rule.
Injectivity gives zero, and division by a unit
#{Z_{\mathrm{pure}}} preserves zero.}
}

\p{A weight depending only on elementary plaquette
holonomies, including #{w=1}, has the stated twist
invariance. If it is a product of class functions,
it is gauge invariant as well. A general
gauge-invariant weight need not have the additional
twist invariance.}
